The previous example handles the case for goroutines receiving on a channel nicely,
but what if we’re dealing with the reverse situation: a goroutine blocked on attempt‐
ing to write a value to a channel? Here’s a quick example to demonstrate the issue:
newRandStream := func() <-chan int {
 randStream := make(chan int)
 go func() {
 defer fmt.Println("newRandStream closure exited.")
 defer close(randStream)
 for {
 randStream <- rand.Int()
 }
 }()
 return randStream
}
randStream := newRandStream()
fmt.Println("3 random ints:")
for i := 1; i <= 3; i++ {
 fmt.Printf("%d: %d\n", i, <-randStream)
}
Here we print out a message when the goroutine successfully terminates.
Running this code produces:
3 random ints:
1: 5577006791947779410
2: 8674665223082153551
3: 6129484611666145821
You can see from the output that the deferred fmt.Println statement never gets run.
After the third iteration of our loop, our goroutine blocks trying to send the next ran‐
dom integer to a channel that is no longer being read from. We have no way of telling
the producer it can stop. The solution, just like for the receiving case, is to provide the
producer goroutine with a channel informing it to exit:
newRandStream := func(done <-chan interface{}) <-chan int {
 randStream := make(chan int)
 go func() {
 defer fmt.Println("newRandStream closure exited.")
 defer close(randStream)
 for {
 select {
 case randStream <- rand.Int():
 case <-done:
 return
 }
 }
 }()
